  {
    name: "Oxia Planum",
    terrainType: ["Low-relief, clay-bearing plain with fractured bedrock, on the boundary between the cratered southern highlands of Arabia Terra and the smooth northern lowlands of Chryse Planitia. About 1% of the surface is covered by 396 isolated upstanding mounds. Erosion has exhumed Noachian sedimentary rock, and a 2024 high-resolution geological map identifies 15 distinct units."],
    // Approximate mean equatorial/low-latitude Mars surface temperature. This is a general
    // climatological estimate, NOT taken from the sources below. The daily swing is large
    // (roughly tens of degrees C), so check a site-specific thermal model before relying on it.
    temperature: -60,
    surfaceMaterial: ["Fractured Noachian clay-bearing bedrock (a Fe/Mg-rich phyllosilicate-dominated unit) overlain in places by regolith and wind-blown dust. Orbital data distinguish at least two bedrock types in the clay-bearing unit: an orange type in HiRISE images, which matches the strongest clay signals, and a second, spectrally weaker type. Pyroxenes and Al-bearing phyllosilicates occur in the wider catchment. Opal (amorphous hydrated silica) is present stratigraphically between the clay-bearing plains and overlying sedimentary deposits. Grain size and dust cover are not well constrained from orbit."],
    // Qualitative only; not drawn from the sources below.
    sunlight: ["Low-latitude (near-equatorial, northern hemisphere) site, so it gets strong year-round insolation that varies seasonally with Mars's orbital eccentricity and axial tilt. Global or regional dust storms can sharply reduce solar flux for weeks at a time. This matters for any solar-powered mission."],
    // Qualitative only; not drawn from the sources below.
    radiation: ["Typical Mars surface radiation environment: galactic cosmic rays plus occasional solar particle events. It is only partly shielded by the thin atmosphere, though the low elevation gives slightly more shielding than highland sites. Surface rocks are expected to have been exposed to cosmic bombardment only recently, which limits damage to organic matter. Drilling to 2 m is planned to reach material better shielded from radiation and from daily temperature swings."],
    resources: [
      "Fe/Mg-rich clay minerals (phyllosilicates), which are evidence of past liquid water and a good substrate for preserving organics",
      "Opal (hydrated silica), a water-bearing mineral that can preserve biosignatures",
      "Hydrated minerals as a potential water source: water bound in clays and opal. Surface water ice is not reported at this latitude.",
      "Pyroxene-bearing material in the catchment (a possible source of Mg/Fe/Ca silicates)",
    ],
    interestingBits: [
      "Likely the oldest landing site ever visited by a rover on Mars, with Noachian rocks dated to roughly 4 billion years old.",
      "Chosen for the ExoMars Rosalind Franklin rover after a 5-year process that narrowed eight candidate sites down to one.",
      "Recent orbital mapping shows the clay deposits extend far beyond earlier estimates, reaching toward Mawrth Vallis about 300 km away. It may be a surviving fragment of a much larger ancient water system.",
      "A 2026 study suggests Oxia Planum's clays formed first, around 4 billion years ago, predating those at Mawrth Vallis.",
      "Opal deposits near the centre of the landing site are prime targets in the search for past habitable conditions.",
      "Rosalind Franklin will be the first rover to drill 2 m below the surface, to find better-preserved biosignatures and analyse them in its onboard laboratory.",
      "There's an actual landing planned for 2030 (per the 2024 ESA/Open University mapping announcement).",
      "The clay-bearing unit is well exposed within the landing area, including near its centre.",
    ],
    hazards: [
      "Roughly 396 isolated mounds, several of them inside the landing ellipse, which are a landing and traverse hazard.",
      "Fractured bedrock and rocky terrain that could limit rover mobility and landing safety.",
      "Dust storms and dust deposition that reduce solar power and obscure surfaces.",
      "Strong diurnal temperature swings that stress hardware.",
      "Surface radiation, which degrades organics near the surface and is the reason for the 2 m drilling strategy.",
      "Ground-truth uncertainty: much of the mineralogy and morphology comes from orbital data (CRISM, OMEGA, CaSSIS, HiRISE), so regolith thickness, grain size and bearing strength are poorly known.",
    ],
    dataSources: [
      {
        name: "ESA: ExoMars rover targets vast bed of clay in search for life",
        url: "https://www.esa.int/Science_Exploration/Human_and_Robotic_Exploration/ExoMars_rover_targets_vast_bed_of_clay_in_search_for_life",
      },
      {
