  {
  name: "Peace Vallis (Gale Crater, Mars)",
  terrainType: [
    "Ancient stream valley on the northern rim of Gale Crater (about 4.21°S, 137.23°E), roughly 35 km long.",
    "The valley enters the crater through a gap in the rim about 15 km wide and drains a catchment of about 730 km² of upland plains.",
    "It ends in the Peace Vallis alluvial fan, which covers about 80 km² on Aeolis Palus, the low plain in the crater's northern interior.",
    "The fan's average thickness is estimated at roughly 9 m, and numerous inverted (exhumed) channels are visible on its western surface.",
    "The fan's morphology and thermal inertia vary, so orbital data distinguish upper-fan, lower-fan and other alluvial units.",
    "Gale Crater itself is a roughly 154 km impact crater that straddles the hemispheric dichotomy, with Aeolis Mons (Mount Sharp) rising from its centre.",
    "The fan's distal end lies near Curiosity's 2012 landing site (Bradbury Landing). The fan likely onlapped Bradbury Rise and spilled into a topographic low to its east.",
  ],
  // Rough annual-mean surface temperature for an equatorial Gale-latitude site. This is a general
  // climatological estimate, NOT taken from the sources below. The daily swing is on the order of
  // tens of degrees C, so check Curiosity REMS data before relying on it.
  temperature: -50,
  surfaceMaterial: [
    "Fluvially deposited sediment: a debris and sediment apron delivered by stream and groundwater flow, from sand-sized to coarser material. Grain size has not been mapped in detail from the sources reviewed.",
    "Crater-fill units (CF1/CF2) form Aeolis Palus, and mapped alluvial units (AF, BF, CS) form and flank the fan.",
    "Thin sediments, roughly 10–20 m, cap the upper fan and may record a later period of fluvial activity.",
    "The nearby Sheepbed mudstone, which Curiosity analysed, is evidence of a habitable lacustrine environment. Its age relative to the fan is poorly constrained.",
    "Regolith and dust thickness are not quantified in the sources reviewed.",
  ],
  // Qualitative only; not drawn from the sources below.
  sunlight: [
    "Near-equatorial site (about 4°S), so it gets strong insolation year-round, with seasonal variation from Mars's orbital eccentricity.",
    "Global or regional dust storms can cut solar flux sharply for weeks.",
    "Crater walls and Aeolis Mons can shade nearby terrain at low sun angles, though the fan is far from the rim.",
  ],
  // Qualitative only; not drawn from the sources below.
  radiation: [
    "Standard Mars surface environment: galactic cosmic rays plus occasional solar particle events.",
    "Gale's floor lies several kilometres below Mars datum, so it gets somewhat more atmospheric shielding than highland sites.",
    "Curiosity's RAD instrument has measured the real surface dose at Gale. Look up its published values for engineering use.",
  ],
  resources: [
    "Evidence of past water: the fan and valley record sustained fluvial activity, with water probably coming from precipitation, possibly snow.",
    "Possible groundwater springline deposits at the fan, per a 2019 conference abstract (Amazonian-age groundwater, implying a late period of habitability).",
    "Fine-grained sediments and clay-bearing mudstones nearby, which are good targets for organics and a potential source of hydrated minerals.",
    "Abundant loose sediment, which is a possible construction feedstock. Surface water ice is not reported at this latitude.",
  ],
  interestingBits: [
    "Curiosity has already visited the region: it landed at the distal end of the fan on August 6, 2012, so ground truth already exists for its geology.",
    "The IAU adopted the name Peace Vallis on September 26, 2012, after the Peace River in Canada.",
    "It is the largest and best-studied fluvial feature in northern Gale Crater.",
    "Crater counting suggests most alluvial deposits in Gale formed in the Hesperian, so habitable conditions persisted after the Noachian.",
    "Evidence points to possible younger fluvial reactivation of the upper fan, linked to hypothesised snowmelt activity like that in Margaritifer Terra.",
    "High-resolution orbital images suggest at least one glacial episode also affected Gale and its watershed.",
    "A fan-to-mountain traverse is possible: the fan sits in a crater whose central mound exposes a long layered record of Mars's climate history.",
  ],
  hazards: [
    "Rocky, boulder-strewn terrain on the fan and valley that could limit landing safety and rover mobility.",
    "Steep slopes at the crater rim and valley walls, and rough inverted-channel ridges on the fan surface.",
    "Landing-ellipse risk near Gale's rim and Aeolis Mons, where rough terrain nearby adds uncertainty.",
    "Dust storms that reduce solar power and obscure surfaces.",
    "Large daily temperature swings that stress hardware.",
    "Wheel damage: Curiosity's wheels took heavy wear on abrasive, sharp-edged rocks in Gale, so traverse planning matters.",
    "Surface radiation, which degrades organics near the surface.",
  ],
  dataSources: [
